// expect: runtime_error
// expect-error: E1000

// GAP — owner: KI-090, High, 0.0.8 (item C7 / D5).
// Measured 2026-09-11 on the VM: error[E1000] wrong number of arguments:
// want=2, got=1.
//
// NOTE: this file compiles. The gap is at run time, which is why it lives here
// rather than in ../compile/ — a compile-only snapshot records it as `ok` and
// shows nothing.
//
// DO NOT RUN THIS NATIVELY. The native backend segfaults on it. The VM-only
// runtime snapshot is the pin; the native side is registered as a skipped
// parity fixture instead.
//
// Passing an *unconstrained* function as a value works —
// ../../working/accepts/infer_higher_order_apply.flx does exactly that. What
// fails is passing a *constrained* one. `dbl` is lowered with its dictionary
// prepended, so it is arity 2, but `twice` stores it as a value of type
// `(a) -> a` and calls it with one argument.
//
// The Core is provably right: it reads
// `λ%t569. dbl(__dict_m8_466C6F772E4E756D_Num_Int, %t569)`, which is the
// partial application that should work. Both backends still reject it — the
// dictionary never gets bound at the point the value is built.
//
// This is the clearest single demonstration that generics and type classes are
// one problem at the boundary: the generic higher-order machinery is fine, and
// what breaks is the evidence reaching the value.

fn twice<a>(f: (a) -> a, x: a) -> a {
    f(f(x))
}

fn dbl<a: Num>(x: a) -> a {
    x + x
}

fn main() with IO {
    print(twice(dbl, 2))
}
